# Shells, and the small utilities that exist to be used from
# them.

shot    env
size    36 80
for     env getenv printenv checkenv whoami logname su
cap     Seven small programs about the environment and who you are, each
cap     asked for itself.  `env' runs a command with the environment
cap     changed; `getenv' reads one variable back and wants -p before it
cap     will PRINT rather than just set a status (it is DESIGNA's and
cap     answers in German); `checkenv' tests one; `whoami', `logname' and
cap     `su' are about the user, and this session is 0.0.
run     env FOO=bar printenv | grep FOO
wait    8
run     getenv -p TERM
wait    5
run     checkenv TERM
wait    5
run     whoami
wait    5
run     logname
wait    5
run     su -?
wait    6

shot    exist
for     exist checkenv ask ynad
cap     exist answers in its STATUS rather than on the screen -- 0 for a
cap     file that is there, 1 for one that is not -- which is what a shell
cap     script wants and why it looks silent when you run it by hand.
run     exist /dd/SYS/motd; echo "exist says $?"
wait    6
run     exist /dd/SYS/no-such-file; echo "exist says $?"
wait    6
run     checkenv TERM; echo "checkenv says $?"
wait    6
run     ynad < /nil
wait    8

shot    gshell
for     gshell mshell
cap     GSHELL, a shell with a menu, and the other three small shells this
cap     disk keeps beside bash.
run     gshell
wait    8

shot    if
for     if exist ask ynad
cap     if, exist and ask are the pieces a shell script tests with -- OS-9
cap     had no `test' and these are what stood in for it.  This one is
cap     asked for by path because `if' is also a bash KEYWORD, and the
cap     keyword wins: typing `if' alone never reaches the program.
run     /dd/CMDS/if -?
wait    6
run     exist /dd/SYS/motd
wait    5
run     exist /dd/SYS/nothing-here
wait    6

shot    ksh
cap     pd-ksh, the Korn shell, running as a program under bash: its own
cap     prompt, its own builtins.
run     ksh
wait    4
send    \r
wait    1
send    echo; for i in 1 2 3; do echo line $i; done\r
wait    4
send    exit\r
wait    2

shot    printenv
cap     The environment a login on this disk actually arrives with.
run     printenv
wait    4

shot    printf
cap     printf formats rather than echoes, and it WORKS -- with one flaw:
cap     the literal text before the FIRST conversion is dropped.  Between
cap     and after conversions everything is right, which is why the first
cap     two lines below are perfect and the third loses only its leading
cap     `a'.  Begin the format with a conversion and nothing is lost.  A
cap     format with no conversion at all is entirely "before the first
cap     conversion", so it prints nothing -- the last line here.
run     printf "%d %s %d\n" 4 "is bigger than " 3
wait    5
run     printf "%dab\n" 7
wait    4
run     printf "a%db%dc\n" 1 2
wait    4
run     printf "hello\n" x
wait    4
run     echo "echo has no such flaw"
wait    5

shot    sh
cap     sh, a small Bourne shell -- and the one with a real OS-9 chd, so
cap     pwd moves when you ask it to.
run     sh
wait    4
send    echo hello from sh; pwd\r
wait    4
send    exit\r
wait    2

shot    whoami
for     whoami logname tty su
cap     Who you are, twice, and it is worth reading closely: `whoami' and
cap     `logname' answer `su' because they look you up by USER NUMBER in
cap     SYS/password -- everything here is owned 0.0 -- while $USER, which
cap     SYS/login set, says tester.  `tty' asks the path itself and answers
cap     `term', the name of the console device.
run     whoami
wait    4
run     logname
wait    4
run     echo "USER is $USER, and the terminal is:"
wait    4
run     tty
wait    5


shot    bash
for     bash expr
cap     The shell this disk hands you, answering for itself: its version,
cap     an arithmetic expansion, and `expr' beside it doing the same sum
cap     the way a script written for OS-9's own shell would have to.
run     echo $BASH_VERSION
wait    6
run     echo $((355/113)) $((7*6))
wait    6
run     expr 355 / 113
wait    6
run     expr 7 \* 6
wait    8
run     bash --version < /nil
wait    8

shot    hist
for     hist xc run su
cap     Four small shell helpers, each wanting something the shell does not
cap     hand it.  `hist' is meant to be C-shell history and line editing and
cap     prints a rule of asterisks and exits.  `xc' runs the commands marked
cap     in a file and refuses to start without HOME.  `run' rebinds a
cap     program's stdio to a terminal named by PORT and says so when there
cap     is none.  `su' is GNU su, and answers --help rather than -?.
run     xc -?
wait    6
run     run -?
wait    6
run     su --help | head -n 5
wait    10
run     hist
wait    6

